<p>A two-week ceasefire between the United States and Iran has opened a narrow path for diplomacy, but shipping through the Strait of Hormuz remains slow and uncertain as Tehran and Washington publicly clash over the waterway’s status and terms. Iran has tied resumed transit to coordination with its armed forces and floated tolls for safe passage, while the White House says the ceasefire requires the strait to reopen safely and without Iranian tolls. The standoff matters far beyond the Gulf because the strait is a crucial route for global oil and gas shipments. Even after the ceasefire, only a small number of vessels have tried to cross, some have turned back, and major shipping companies are still moving cautiously. Still, there is a modest bright spot: the ceasefire has shifted the crisis away from open fighting and toward talks, with negotiations expected in Islamabad on April 10. For now, though, governments and industry groups want clearer rules and fully unimpeded navigation before trade can return to normal.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/1IOZNQ0xSabTrNqnRIj5S0N0WDFulNNpM8mQAmDGcppx2Dhg04zIyVP7Wce2_mzmbamz5UgqC2PQWCLOxpuAwNlIrq4DQfEh6rFQpPUmkz1uE0YD_8KYUNux9AZN1Gp4lqa8geCcwoor6uaspi45Sqcu0EcZV5dHXsIDnr8jsvNSaJtCIYAiPxmAdHU6_qW4478' alt='Vessels off the coast of Oman on Wednesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Toll backlash: Greek Prime Minister Kyriakos Mitsotakis said charging ships to cross Hormuz would be unacceptable and would set a dangerous precedent for freedom of navigation.</li><li>Industry pressure: Oil executives told Politico they were lobbying the White House and State Department, warning that an Iranian toll could raise costs and expose companies to sanctions risks.</li><li>Alternate channels: Iran’s Revolutionary Guard announced designated alternative routes for ships, saying sea-mine risks threatened the main shipping lane.</li><li>Asian testing: Chinese tankers joined the queue to test the route after the truce, though several vessels reportedly turned back at the last moment.</li><li>Indian backlog: More than 25 India-bound ships were still stuck as Indian operators waited for government clearance before transiting the strait.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>White House: The administration said reports that the Strait of Hormuz was closed were false and maintained that President Trump’s ceasefire terms require the complete, immediate, and safe reopening of the waterway without Iranian tolls. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-live-white-house-holds-briefing-as-trump-agrees-to-two-week-ceasefire-in-iran-war'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Iranian authorities: Tehran said passage would reopen on a conditional basis and under coordination with Iranian armed forces during the truce. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/trump-says-he-has-agreed-to-suspend-bombing-and-attack-of-iran-for-two-weeks/article70836115.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Shipping companies: Maersk said it would take a cautious approach to Hormuz transits and had no clear security timeline for a return to normal operations, while Hapag-Lloyd said recovery could take six to eight weeks. (<a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/maersk-says-us-iran-ceasefire-may-create-strait-hormuz-transit-opportunities-2026-04-08/'>Reuters</a>)</li><li>Gulf states: Regional experts told Al Jazeera that Gulf governments are uneasy about any deal that leaves Iran with lasting leverage over a route central to their energy exports. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/9/for-gulf-states-hormuz-uncertainty-casts-shadow-over-fragile-us-iran-truce'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Oil industry: Executives argued that accepting tolls would raise cargo costs, create legal exposure under sanctions rules, and encourage similar demands at other strategic chokepoints. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/04/08/hormuz-oil-trump-iran-tolls-00863570'>Politico</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https:/